{"ts":"2026-09-07T11:08:42.695Z","author":"claude","author_source":"asserted","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"ac5fbc41d1fa70d2dee4261b","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"implementer","source":"argv"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.263","source":"probe"}},"op":"create","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/id","value":"pmc-6eau"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/title","value":"A test asserts an error that only occurs when no package.json exists anywhere above the OS temp directory, so it fails on a host where one does"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/description","value":"test/release-context.test.ts, 'throws when release-version-from-package finds no package.json', creates a fixture under tmpdir() and asserts resolveReleaseContext walks to the filesystem root without finding a package.json. Its own comment states the assumption out loud: '/tmp itself has none.'\n\nThat is an assumption about the HOST, not about the code. On this development machine /tmp/package.json existed - 60 bytes, created 2026-09-06 by an npx/bunx execution probe, exactly the kind of thing that leaves one behind - so the walk found it and the assertion failed with a message that reads like a production regression:\n\n  The input did not match /requires a package.json in the current directory or an ancestor/.\n  Input: 'Error: /tmp/package.json does not contain a valid version field'\n\nConfirmed pre-existing and environmental: the failure reproduces on origin/main with all local changes stashed, and disappears once the stray file is moved aside. CI has never seen it because a fresh runner has a clean /tmp.\n\nTwo separable problems. The verdict depends on state nobody declared or controls, so it can flip with no commit - the same class as a gate that measures the clock. And when it flips, the failure names the production regex rather than the unmet precondition, so the reader's first hypothesis is a code change that did not happen; this session spent time confirming it was not one.\n\nNeither weakening the assertion nor skipping on an unsuitable host is acceptable, because a skipped test reports as a pass. The test should assert its precondition explicitly so an unsuitable host reports ITSELF, or the search should take a boundary so the case is reachable without depending on the entire filesystem above the fixture."},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/type","value":"Issue"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/status","value":"open"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/priority","value":2},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/tags","value":["flake","testing"]},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/created_at","value":"2026-09-07T11:08:42.695Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-09-07T11:08:42.695Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/author","value":"claude"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/acceptance_criteria","value":"The test's verdict does not depend on the contents of directories above the fixture; On a host where the precondition does not hold, the failure names the precondition, not the production regex"}],"before_hash":"3cc22dff72be7b14824654a7a64ea62b04799939b2fee54c1b5f52ca60bf6df0","after_hash":"09fc2cedaf49342c3e9f2c9df6f5ba92eeabacedb2fad3512348e050aa4577bf","item_hash_version":3,"message":"","event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"0439eb7d0fc650ebf2d10c6ced6b19997308f114525dcfa998e8b71134eb3a48"}
